00:02:55.730 --> 00:03:00.130
So when we are looking at our chemical
recipes, meaning our different

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:02.230
chemical reactions that we will see.

00:03:02.575 --> 00:03:07.015
Generally, in general chemistry,
you will see five major types.

00:03:07.335 --> 00:03:08.445
So what are they?

00:03:08.925 --> 00:03:13.505
The first one is a combination
reaction, meaning you will generally

00:03:13.505 --> 00:03:16.865
see it as A plus B is equal to AB.

00:03:17.645 --> 00:03:20.849
This is going to make more sense
when we go through our examples.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.640
So what we do is we assign
each species a letter.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:27.970
So we have one, two species
being combined together.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:32.920
You have decomposition, which
is a B is leading to a plus B.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:39.059
You have single replacement, which
is a plus B C leads to a C plus

00:03:39.059 --> 00:03:44.529
B double this double replacement
or displacement is a B plus CD.

00:03:44.589 --> 00:03:48.410
And notice how each swap or each
swap with each other or trade play

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:51.754
trade places, which is a D plus CB.

00:03:52.605 --> 00:03:57.445
And then you have our combustion reaction,
which kind of doesn't really follow any

00:03:57.445 --> 00:04:03.504
letters, but it does always have the same
products and the same major ingredients

00:04:03.955 --> 00:04:08.664
where we have a hydrocarbon combined
with oxygen gas to make CO2 and water.

00:04:09.305 --> 00:04:10.434
So these are our five types.

00:04:10.844 --> 00:04:13.925
So what we're going to do is
we're going to identify our

00:04:13.925 --> 00:04:15.535
reaction to help us predict.

00:04:16.045 --> 00:04:19.394
We will go through our swap drop
and then we will go through our

00:04:19.394 --> 00:04:21.425
balancing act in order to do these.

00:04:21.729 --> 00:04:24.820
So, stick around and let's go through
all those different examples together.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:28.219
Okay, so in our first reaction
that we are going to be

00:04:28.219 --> 00:04:32.059
practicing, we have H2 plus O2 gas.

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:34.960
How exactly are we going to do this?

00:04:34.969 --> 00:04:39.349
Well, the general idea is we need to
assign letters to each of our species.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.669
So that way we can determine
which recipe we have.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:45.909
So on our first element
we have hydrogen, right?

00:04:45.909 --> 00:04:47.530
So hydrogen is an element by itself.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.539
So what we are going to do is we
are going to label this the letter A

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:53.919
because it's the only element here.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:56.580
We don't bother with
anything with the subscripts.

00:04:56.609 --> 00:05:01.650
We're just labeling each type of species
we see or each cation, each anion.

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:05.360
Every individual species of
hydrogen gas is our species

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.740
here on our next letter, right?

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:13.230
We see that we have oxygen So this is
going to be a B in this case being that

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:19.760
once again, it is by itself So that's
going to be our next species So now what

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:23.960
we're going to look at is trying to figure
out which chemical recipe does that match?

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:29.329
So when we do that receipt with our
first one here is that the combination

00:05:29.329 --> 00:05:34.620
reaction is a plus B is equal to a b.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:37.830
So that's going to be
what we will have here.

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:41.470
So when we are doing this,
we'll write that down for

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:43.719
ourselves is write down our a b.

00:05:44.399 --> 00:05:48.750
So we have a and b combined together.

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:53.840
So what we are then going to do
is we're not just going to just

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.180
combine everything together.

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:56.970
So you don't want to just write.

00:05:58.205 --> 00:06:00.125
H2O2 and you're good.

00:06:00.305 --> 00:06:02.565
It's, that is a big, that's a big no.

00:06:02.635 --> 00:06:04.295
You don't want to just write that down.

00:06:05.075 --> 00:06:08.495
We are going to write each
individual species first.

00:06:08.495 --> 00:06:13.115
So we have H as one and then we have O.

00:06:13.145 --> 00:06:15.004
Now is that the correct answer?

00:06:15.045 --> 00:06:18.535
And it looks really, really
ugly and hideous at the moment.

00:06:18.535 --> 00:06:19.805
It's not quite right.

00:06:20.535 --> 00:06:23.555
So what we need to do is we
need to either swap drop.

00:06:24.205 --> 00:06:27.715
However, both H and O
together is covalent.

00:06:28.145 --> 00:06:30.795
So what we need to do is
go off our bonding trends.

00:06:30.825 --> 00:06:34.895
If you remember, oxygen makes
two bonds, hydrogen makes one.

00:06:35.405 --> 00:06:42.794
So what would we have here is H2O is
going to be our product in this case.

00:06:42.794 --> 00:06:43.794
So you're going to make water.

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:48.230
So that would be our
first chemical reaction.

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:52.390
Now the, our last step that
we're going to be doing is

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:54.510
we're going to be balancing it.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:07:00.009
So we have H2 and O2
and H2 plus O2 is H2O.

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:02.230
So is it balanced?

00:07:02.239 --> 00:07:03.330
Well, let's take a look.

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:06.920
So we look on the left
and the right side here.

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:09.120
We have two oxygens.

00:07:09.715 --> 00:07:12.725
That are in two hydrogens
now on the right side.

00:07:12.725 --> 00:07:14.425
We have two hydrogens, one oxygen.

00:07:14.425 --> 00:07:16.425
So we see there's it's not quite right.

00:07:17.224 --> 00:07:24.055
So we will add a two in front of water
to multiply both of the hydrogen, oxygen,

00:07:24.724 --> 00:07:29.264
and then we will put a two in front of
hydrogen to balance out the two sides.

00:07:29.274 --> 00:07:34.715
See, kind of like zigzag back and forth
is the method I usually kind of revert to.

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:38.990
The C is everything on the two sides.

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:43.100
So we see we have four hydrogens,
two oxygens, everything, it's great.

00:07:43.630 --> 00:07:45.690
Let's go on to the next example.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:47.180
All right, so our next one.

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:52.600
So here again, we have beryllium plus,
uh, hydrofluoric acid adding together.

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:56.440
So what we're going to do right
now is assign our letters.

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:59.710
So our first one, right,
beryllium is all by itself.

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:01.430
So we'll give that the letter A.

00:08:01.985 --> 00:08:09.125
Then we have H and F, so that's going
to be, H would be a B in this case,

00:08:09.685 --> 00:08:13.155
and then with uh, fluorine would be C.

00:08:13.664 --> 00:08:18.105
Um, even though these are covalent,
we could, you could let the letter,

00:08:18.114 --> 00:08:22.475
the, the uh, their charges kind
of guide you here if it helps you

00:08:22.495 --> 00:08:24.525
see the two different species.

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:29.430
Being that hydrogen is usually a
plus and, and fluorine is a minus,

00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:33.500
that is one way to help you see
it, but they are still covalent.

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:37.269
So being that we have A plus
BC, all right, which reaction

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:38.530
are we going to follow?

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:40.409
So we are looking for A plus BC.

00:08:41.039 --> 00:08:45.620
And what we see here is right
here in the middle is A plus BC.

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:49.670
So what we're going to
write down is our recipe.

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:58.380
And it says that, okay, A plus
B C is equal to A C plus B.

00:08:58.599 --> 00:09:00.660
Alright, so let's follow that.

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:04.659
So now what we are looking
for is what is our A species?

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:09.339
Well, we have beryllium, and if we want
to think of it as being that we have to

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:12.630
swap drop this, we will write the charges.

00:09:13.284 --> 00:09:15.734
So we have two plus for beryllium.

00:09:16.444 --> 00:09:22.285
Then we have fluorine, which
is a minus one charge, and then

00:09:22.295 --> 00:09:25.745
that's going to be plus hydrogen.

00:09:26.955 --> 00:09:30.335
Now to keep in mind, we got to
remember is what is the natural way

00:09:30.335 --> 00:09:32.834
that hydrogen is created, right?

00:09:32.854 --> 00:09:34.514
What's its natural state?

00:09:34.875 --> 00:09:37.655
This is one of your seven diatomic gases.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:44.130
So being that this is a 7, or one of
the diatomic 7, we need to write this

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:48.869
as H2, and if you're not sure what
I'm talking about about those diatomic

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:53.200
gases, refer to my diatomic gas video,
it will help you remember those.

00:09:54.125 --> 00:09:57.455
Alright, so, now for step
two, which is swap drop.

00:09:57.925 --> 00:10:02.655
We see that with beryllium and
fluorine, it's not balanced charge

00:10:02.664 --> 00:10:04.995
wise, they need to be neutral.

00:10:05.505 --> 00:10:09.394
So what we're going to do is we're
going to bring the two from beryllium

00:10:09.405 --> 00:10:17.545
down and make a BEF2 plus H2, okay,
and then that's going to be a balanced

00:10:17.545 --> 00:10:20.265
chemical equation in terms of charge.

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:26.030
So, now the last step is making
sure that, okay, well, are all of

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:31.409
our coefficients, subscripts, is
everything balanced in this problem?

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:34.659
And one thing you're going to
kind of quickly notice is that on

00:10:34.659 --> 00:10:39.000
the right hand side we have two
hydrogens, on the left we have one.

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:40.820
It's not quite right.

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:46.980
So what we will do is we'll put
a two on the left, and then that

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:48.850
balances not only the hydrogens.

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:53.630
But it also balances what it's next
to, which is the fluorines as well.

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:59.400
Okay, so it does all of it at one, so
this is our balanced chemical reaction.

00:10:59.819 --> 00:11:02.650
Usually the single replacement
ones are really, really easy

00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:03.810
to balance most of the time.

00:11:03.819 --> 00:11:06.920
Let's predict and balance the
following chemical reaction.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:11.780
We have calcium hydroxide
reacting with phosphoric acid.

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:14.810
So this is an acid base reaction.

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:16.819
So how can we label this?

00:11:16.819 --> 00:11:19.360
Well, just like any,
whenever it's acid base.

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:22.670
You're going to kind of see
the same general trend here.

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:27.719
So what we will do is we will label
all of our cations and anions.

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:34.420
One trick that is really helpful is label
your ions before you swap drop anything.

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:39.620
So realizing calcium is two plus
hydroxide is a polyatomic that's minus.

00:11:40.205 --> 00:11:43.275
Hydrogen's a plus,
phosphate's a three minus.

00:11:43.555 --> 00:11:48.275
If you label it at the start, it
makes your process a lot easier later.

00:11:48.604 --> 00:11:49.314
So keep that in mind.

00:11:49.324 --> 00:11:53.134
It also helps you see the letters,
which is also really good, right?

00:11:53.134 --> 00:11:54.594
So we have calcium, right?

00:11:54.594 --> 00:11:54.805
That's A.

00:11:54.805 --> 00:11:57.014
Hydroxide is B.

00:11:57.814 --> 00:11:59.824
Then we have our new species over here.

00:11:59.824 --> 00:12:01.734
We have hydrogen and then phosphate.

00:12:01.785 --> 00:12:05.564
So we see AB plus CD.

00:12:06.224 --> 00:12:09.415
So what we are looking for is,
okay, well, what quite matches that?

00:12:09.814 --> 00:12:16.184
And we see that this double displacement
reactions will match that recipe here.

00:12:16.854 --> 00:12:19.045
So this is going to be our answer.

00:12:19.544 --> 00:12:24.984
So A B plus C D is going
to equal to A D plus C B.

00:12:25.514 --> 00:12:27.494
So let's write that down for ourselves.

00:12:28.074 --> 00:12:32.665
So we have A D plus C B.

00:12:33.334 --> 00:12:33.844
All right.

00:12:33.844 --> 00:12:35.124
So what is A?

00:12:35.165 --> 00:12:40.114
So we will have calcium is
going to be our first one.

00:12:40.114 --> 00:12:44.535
So we have calcium two plus that's
going to combine with phosphate.

00:12:44.605 --> 00:12:45.775
So we'll use a different color here.

00:12:45.775 --> 00:12:47.115
So P O four.

00:12:47.704 --> 00:12:49.015
Three minus.

00:12:49.514 --> 00:12:52.415
So one thing that you're going to
kind of notice is that, okay, we

00:12:52.415 --> 00:12:54.344
have to swap drop these, right?

00:12:54.344 --> 00:12:56.574
Cause it's not charge balanced.

00:12:57.374 --> 00:13:01.004
So the three is of the charge is
going to come next to calcium.

00:13:01.704 --> 00:13:08.875
And then we will put the phosphate in
parentheses and put a two on the outside.

00:13:08.875 --> 00:13:12.324
So let's kind of highlight those
in their respective colors so we

00:13:12.324 --> 00:13:14.915
can see where those swap dropped.

00:13:14.944 --> 00:13:15.385
Okay.

00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:20.469
Some classes may also call it a swappy
swappy because we're treating those

00:13:20.469 --> 00:13:24.569
two charges now Our next part is C B.

00:13:24.610 --> 00:13:32.499
So we have hydrogen which is H
plus and then B is O H Minus.

00:13:33.019 --> 00:13:39.970
Alright, so O H minus All right So
this is our first part One thing

00:13:39.970 --> 00:13:44.140
you might notice is if you kind of
look over here on the right hand

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:47.069
side H O H What does that exactly?

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:48.850
look like?

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:53.990
Well, when we have H O H written,
it's really handy to write

00:13:54.020 --> 00:13:55.680
that to help you balance it.

00:13:56.470 --> 00:14:01.210
Um, but when we kind of
summarize it later, it's going

00:14:01.210 --> 00:14:04.129
to be written as H 2 O, right?

00:14:04.130 --> 00:14:05.709
Cause that's what it actually is.

00:14:06.279 --> 00:14:10.160
So, but I'm going to leave it like
that to show you a balancing trick.

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:16.950
One of the things that students, whenever
they see polyatomic, uh, problems, they

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:21.595
always kind of freak out a little bit
because they do look like one of the most

00:14:21.595 --> 00:14:28.665
complicated problems that you can possibly
see, but there is a hidden trick behind

00:14:28.665 --> 00:14:32.594
them that makes them extremely easy to do.

00:14:32.994 --> 00:14:38.455
And that is the fact that the polyatomics
are on both sides of the equation.

00:14:38.755 --> 00:14:41.905
So I just highlighted them
in their respective colors.

00:14:42.415 --> 00:14:47.215
So you see hydroxide is in purple,
and then you have phosphate, which

00:14:47.215 --> 00:14:52.784
is in red, and it's on both the
left and the right hand side of the

00:14:52.785 --> 00:14:56.709
equation, which is Really, really nice.

00:14:57.040 --> 00:15:00.829
So what we are going to use
to balance this is what I

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:03.840
refer to as the zigzag method.

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:08.099
I don't know if anyone's
actually, if anyone's named this

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:09.380
one, but this is how I name it.

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:14.120
And it's all about going
back and forth to balance.

00:15:14.619 --> 00:15:18.119
With the zigzag method, what we're going
to first start with is we're going to

00:15:18.119 --> 00:15:20.479
start with the phosphates in this case.

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:26.014
So what we see here is on the
right hand side, we have two

00:15:26.165 --> 00:15:28.175
phosphates in our formula.

00:15:28.594 --> 00:15:33.394
So we got two right here and
we only have one on the left.

00:15:33.814 --> 00:15:38.395
So what we are going to do is put
a two in front of the less species.

00:15:39.044 --> 00:15:44.324
So what we now have is two phosphates
and what we will then do is zigzag

00:15:44.845 --> 00:15:47.185
to the hydrogens and look at them.

